// NEGATIVE TEST — a range pattern's endpoints are an ORDERED literal kind (N6-03),
// and a string is not one.
//
// The normative production spells an endpoint `expression`, which no pattern
// position in this compiler can evaluate; what a range needs is an ORDER.
//
// THERE ARE TWO ORDERED KINDS NOW, NOT ONE. This file's comment used to say the
// integer was "the only ordered literal this language has today" and that `char`
// "waits on N4-04, which owes `char` as a type at all". N4-04 is `satisfied` and
// a char literal is a pattern as of the char-pattern round, so `'a'..='z'` is
// legal and witnessed by tests/06_char_patterns.pd. What is refused here is
// unchanged and for an unchanged reason: a STRING range would need an ordering
// this language has never defined, and lowering it would compare address values
// in C, which answers without meaning anything.
fn main() {
    let v = match 4 {
        "a".."z" => 1,
        _ => 0,
    };
    print_int(v);
}
